Sat down at his desk. Clicked open his laptop. Opened up his email. One broadcast. Didn’t remember signing up. Blog he didn’t know well. Saw a word he didn’t know. Opened up his browser. Searched “fulgent”. 

adjective [literary]

shining brightly. 


Took note. Re-parsed the sentence. Thought less of its sender. Understood it meant “good”. Understood it also meant old. Good in an old way. Closed the tab. Opened another. No question sprang to mind. Background stunning; waterfall. Into rainforest pool. Began to be angry. “Why am I here and some photographer there,” this sort of thing. Felt silly about the anger. Gave up. Closed the laptop. Sipped from metal water-bottle. Old gift. Had the smell of an old water bottle. Benign microbes cling to the rubber seal. Felt a tightness in his chest. Pain in his chest. Two seconds of real pain. Dissipated. Felt silly for panicking. Acid reflux or something. Began to be angry. “Why won’t even that happen to me,” this sort of thing. Felt silly for being angry. Felt bad. “Lot of people would love my life,” this sort of thing. Long afternoon. Nothing to do in front of him. 

Writing is not about these parts of life. Don’t memorialise the banal. Easy to forget, writing, that there are people, reading, who cannot tease substance out from style. If you allow this to happen you will make the world worse and poorer with each keystroke. Trust that disaffected writers have stripped the vein of its treasure entirely. Stop, and look back at the canon of banality. No attitude has maintained such a hold on literary fiction in the past 50 years as disenfranchised haught: to illustrate, I'll pick, from thousands, about one literary bestseller (often a bestseller even in translation, since the Anglophone press imports foreign misery far more readily than foreign joy) from each decade in which an apathetic writer imagines, expounds upon, and advertises apathy. Journey To The End of Night comes out in ‘32, L’Etranger in ‘42, No Longer Human in ‘48, Catcher In The Rye in ‘51, Revolutionary Road in ‘62, Amis’ first, The Rachel Papers, in ‘72, Easton Ellis’ first, Less Than Zero in ‘85, Houellebecq’s first, Whatever, in ‘94, The Pale King written by 2008, Moshfegh’s Rest and Relaxation in 2018. Of course you are sometimes bored. There is something boring about everything. Do not write about boredom. Some of these examples are less ethical than others. The Rachel Papers is not really a social ill being an entertainment at least. No Longer Human is a smear against Gutenberg, however: the most counterrevolutionary, reactionary conservative action is surely to propagandise despair. Do not, especially do not, write about boredom at great length, as an undefeatable opponent; this memetic reproduction of misery is a sadism degrees above the sadism of the conventional horror writer. That The Pale King killed the man who wrote it is not a statement supported properly by fact. That it has had a hand in killing others, or led readers against hope is — with millions of copies sold — irrefutably true. To turn the talent and work of your style toward the emptiness of your experience is one of the few properly unethical uses of art.

The story of Chris Fogle, halfway into The Pale King, comprises one of the only moments of entire lucidity in the book. This is a story of ineffectual time; our wastoid hero tells us “My essential response to everything was ‘Whatever.” The rest of the story expands. He goes through the motions, an accidental nihilist, and “instead of reading something [he would] count the words in it, as though reading was the same as just counting the words.” That is a manifesto against apathy, surely. Wallace’s idiots are always totally aware of the ways they fail at being reasonable people. Fogle describes his own “narcissistic despair.” The alternative he presents is his new life in “the Service”, an IRS career. “Join the side that always, always gets paid”, another character mentions. Fogle joins the IRS the same way Pratchett’s Mort signs up with death, or Saint Christopher with Christ. 

This brings us to the crisis of nihilistic writing. We don’t take Wallace’s “if you can’t beat them” praise of the IRS in earnest, just as Houellebecq’s Whatever does not really make a case for very much, no matter what deliverance emerges in its last few pages. The instruction permitted by major forms is rarely successful: The Great Gatsby, by virtue of brilliant style (which is always more mnemonically powerful than form), has not entered the cultural imagination as a castigation of excess or dishonesty, but a treasure-box of beautiful and romantic scenes. Its current West End run is billed as “The Greatest Party on the Biggest Stage”. Say “Brideshead” to anybody; ostensibly, in the long view, a moralising novel, yet its lasting cultural impression, and the one that will always meet its invocation, is of champagne and picnics, not delirium tremens in a monastery. In an ironic turn, I find that moral tales about excess are more often invoked to describe the initial joy of the proscribed excess. Everything becomes good fun, narrative memory has a sort of Midas touch (by which I mean that it turns bad things into terrific, golden things, of course, not that it is cursed to kill its children and starve to death).This isn’t necessarily a disqualifying negative, but Houellebecq’s protagonists never even have a good time. Even when they are, say, carousing with teenagers, cheating on their wives, or writing about Huysmans. So why write a cautionary tale of worldliness without either a functional caution or a pleasurable excess? 

Houellebecq’s protagonist in Whatever comes clean about just why.

At times, too, I've had the impression that I'd manage to feel quite at home in a life of vacuity. That the relatively painless boredom would enable me to go on making the usual gestures of life. Another big mistake. Prolonged boredom is not tenable as a position: sooner or later it is transformed into feelings that are acutely more painful, of true pain; this is precisely what's happening to me.

Houellebecq wrote a self-help and writing advice essay: “How To Stay Alive”. Here he is honest in his philosophy. A young writer must forgo the world, and all its feelings except pain:

Cultivate hate of self. Hate yourself, disregard others. Hate others, disregard yourself. 

There is a shock tactic here, of course, but the lineage of Celine is not a straight line, and it is more than public decency offended by his declarations that love is an abyss, and that the poet is formed in moments of sexual humiliation and grief, rather than joy or beauty. Seeing what Houellebecq has printed as advice to young men, one wonders what writing, if not this, is worth prosecuting. David Foster Wallace does not want you to join the IRS and put yourself together. Houellebecq does not want you to go out into the woods and realise your place in the world. Much is made of each of the interpersonal villainies of these men: these are misunderstood as blights on otherwise good writing. The lives are/were often morally weak, yes, but the writing is, as the record of a confident weakness, properly evil. Houellebecq will continue to write novels about the suffering inherent to sexual politics and social life. Three hundred pages of sleazy, anhedonic drudgery will pass, before he delivers his protagonists from it all in a flash of revelation. Then he will start writing the next one, until he dies or has a realisation. His own advice to the writer precludes the actual gathering of experience that could contradict his misery: “You do not really have that much more to do with life.” What cowardice. What he calls unavoidable, the exaltation of “holy suffering”, is really quite an artificial, deliberate rejection.

To say a book is unethical because of the damage it causes to others, as I have said of The Pale King, would be to throw The Sorrows of Young Werther, the most famous example of a book inspiring suicides, out with the bathwater. Certainly, Werther (ignoring the artificial and Gatsby-like frame narrative that condemns Werther’s suicide as foolish) denies the world, but at least it supposes something better: love. The modern strain of world-denying cynicism does not even have this otherworldly value to hold above banality. So with no worthy Platonic lie, no caution, and no pleasure, we are left with a gutted version of the Prodigal Son, who leaves home to smoke mids and seethe impotently in a bedsit, and comes home to silently resent a wife. Trade in one species of apathy for another.

It is difficult not to want to poison this kind of Socrates. If Socrates stood up in the square and said you could only live truthfully if you hated the world, and that this hatred was the whole of the truth, who would mourn him? Houellebecq writes this proudly, and claims it gives him more artistic validity. Schopenhauer is dead in Philosophy departments, but his disciples populate fiction so broadly that the roots have been obscured altogether. Fascism hasn't been an openly strong force in mainstream politics for decades, at time of printing, but Hamsun and Celine are still dominant sires in the lineage of literary fiction. The idea that art has no political, ethical dimension was not one Wilde could stand by in his own later life — why have we taken it as given?

There are gestures away from the trap. Dostoevsky is one way, Pessoa another: The Book of Disquiet is a principled book which takes boredom as its central subject, but does not hate the world, and the central vein of Dostoevsky's major novels is the incapacity of nihilism to properly explain or manage living. DFW had a clear idea of good writing. He wrote about it more than almost anything else (e.g. “good writing locates and revives that which is human… really good fiction depicts the world and illuminates the possibility to be alive and human in it…”) and yet he couldn't do it. Heidegger privileges Hölderlin because Hölderlin does not seek to deliver his reader from the world; instead, his images have their roots in life. David Foster Wallace claims to love the world, but gave in to cheap and pessimistic Platonism too often. Houellebecq wants to write like a dog, but instead he writes like a cult leader. Like Saint Paul, if Saint Paul was involved in the whole thing ironically.

Apathy is a default position, as is misanthropy; one to which there is a factive, positive alternative, and one which will happily scoop up your writing for itself if you let it. So pick a team, or know that abstinence is a position. The quality of your life is mostly economic. Magazines can't impact economic policy. The ethics of the society you live in are complicated, and you are at least as disenfranchised as everyone else. You don't have any power except aesthetic power. So consider the ethics — not the politics, but the most fundamental ethics — of the literature you're reading, and that you're writing. And point people into the world, or at least not into an imagined void.



